# capabilities.topic -- facilities Botlish does not have (or has only in a
# limited form), each documented as absent, with the nearest supported
# alternative. Never describe one of these as available; when the language
# or library gains one, replace its page with the real documentation.

@topic capability.overview
title: Facilities Botlish does not have, and what to use instead
summary: Every facility other languages offer that Botlish lacks or offers only in a limited form has a capability page saying so, with the nearest supported alternative; this page lists them.
index: concepts
alias: capabilities; missing features; absent features; unsupported features; does it exist; what is missing; limitations of the language
keywords: capability, absent, missing, unsupported, limitation, feature, exist, alternative
related: capability.integer-division capability.string-formatting capability.input capability.files capability.http capability.process-exit-status capability.test-timeout capability.concurrency capability.exceptions capability.multi-error-handler capability.lambdas capability.match capability.map-filter capability.null capability.assignment capability.struct-type-import

== HOW TO FIND OUT WHETHER SOMETHING EXISTS
Ask for it as you would name it elsewhere: `-help division`, `-help "exit
status"`, `-help "try catch"`, `-help lambda`. A facility that exists
leads to its concept or standard-library page; one that does not leads to
its capability page (the list below), whose title says "absent" or
"limited" and whose NEAREST ALTERNATIVE section says what to write. A
standard-library function that is not listed by `-help NAMESPACE` (for
example `-help str`) does not exist.

@topic capability.integer-division
title: Integer division: absent (mod exists)
summary: Botlish has no integer division: `/` divides Float64 values only, and there is no `%` or integer division function; `mod` (Euclidean remainder) exists, and bit shifts divide by powers of two.
index: concepts stdlib
alias: division; divide; integer division; quotient; div; / operator; % operator; remainder; modulo operator
keywords: divide, quotient, remainder, mod, shift_right, slash, percent
related: concept.integers concept.floats stdlib./ stdlib.mod stdlib.shift_right task.number-text
match: CORE SEMANTIC TYPE | *Botlish has no integer division*
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | unexpected character "%"*

== STATUS
`/` divides Float64 values (stdlib./) and nothing else: `7 / 2` is
rejected at compile time ("/" divides floating-point numbers, and neither
operand here is one ... Botlish has no integer division). There is no `%`
operator and no `div` function. Int is arbitrary precision, with + - *
and the comparisons.

== WHAT EXISTS
  * mod(a, b): the Euclidean remainder, 0 <= r < abs(b); mod(-7, 3) is 2.
    mod(a, 0) fails at run time (CORE SEMANTIC ARITHMETIC).
  * shift_right(n, k): n divided by 2^k, rounded down; bit_and(n, 2^k - 1)
    is the remainder (row and column of a cell of a 4x4 board, for
    example).
  * Division by any other divisor: repeated subtraction (a recursive
    helper). A number's decimal digits need none: std::int::to_string
    writes them (task.number-text).
  * An approximate quotient through Float64: std::float64::truncate of
    std::float64::from_int(a) / std::float64::from_int(b) is exact only
    while both Ints and the quotient fit in 53 bits.

@example accept
[mod(17, 5), mod(-7, 3), shift_right(17, 2), bit_and(17, 3)]
@caption Remainder and power-of-two division
@value [2, 2, 4, 1]
@end

@example reject
x = 7 / 2
x
@caption There is no integer division
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 1:5
@end

@topic capability.process-exit-status
title: Setting the process exit status: absent
summary: A Botlish program cannot choose its exit status: there is no exit function; an executable (and botlish run) exits 0 when the program completes, 1 only after a run-time error.
index: concepts backends
alias: exit status; set exit code; process exit; exit function; return code
keywords: exit, status, code, process, fail, verdict
related: cli.exit-status cli.run backend.native-executable concept.program-value concept.testing

== STATUS
There is no `exit` operation and no way to make the compiler or an
executable exit with a status the program chooses. A declared error cannot
be raised to the top level deliberately either: every call's errors must be
handled or admitted, and a top-level call has no `errors` clause to admit
them in (rule.unhandled-errors).

== NEAREST ALTERNATIVE
Make the outcome the program's value and test it outside: a golden suite
returns its report (std::test::report(), or a verdict String such as
"GOLDEN PASS: 258 passed") and a script compares the value the compiler's
development run prints ("   value:", cli.output), or what the program
prints itself. An executable prints no value. The compiler's own status is
1 when a program is rejected or fails at run time.

@topic capability.test-timeout
title: Per-test timeouts: absent
summary: The test framework has no timeout: a test that does not terminate hangs the suite; limit work by construction (a depth or step bound passed to the code under test) and time the whole run outside.
index: concepts
alias: test timeout; timeout; hanging test
keywords: timeout, hang, infinite loop, test, limit
related: concept.testing capability.process-exit-status capability.concurrency

== STATUS
stdlib/test.bot runs each registered check to completion, in order, and
nothing interrupts a running computation: std::event_loop's timers wake
tasks that wait for them, never a task that is running. A non-terminating
check stops the whole run.

== NEAREST ALTERNATIVE
Give searches and loops an explicit bound the test can choose (a depth
limit, a step count) and test the bound's behavior too; run suites under an
external timeout (the shell's `timeout`).

@topic capability.concurrency
title: Threads and parallelism: limited (isolated VMs, no shared memory)
summary: Native programs start OS threads with std::thread, each a VM sharing no value with another, talking through std::actor::remote; no async/await, locks or shared mutable memory.
index: concepts
alias: threads; concurrency; parallelism; async; await; async await; multithreading; tasks; scheduler; locks; mutex; shared memory
keywords: thread, concurrent, parallel, async, await, task, lock, channel, mutex, event loop, actor, mailbox, timer, std::thread, std::actor::remote, VM, isolation
related: concept.threads concept.remote-actors concept.coroutines concept.actors stdlib.module.std::thread stdlib.module.std::actor::remote stdlib.module.std::event_loop stdlib.module.std::actor concept.mutable-vector capability.test-timeout backend.overview

== STATUS
On the native backends a program can run in parallel: a `thread fn` of a
module is started with std::thread::start on an OS thread of its own, in a
VM of its own -- its own heap, module statics and installed contexts
(concept.threads). VMs share no value: an initialization value, a result
and every message between them are copied (std::transfer), so there is no
lock, no shared mutable memory, no data race and no async/await. Actors of
different threads talk through std::actor::remote: exported mailboxes,
sendable capabilities, typed calls with replies, bounded queues whose
backpressure waits instead of dropping (concept.remote-actors). At most 64
threads run at once in a process. The Tcl backends (interp, compile) refuse
a program that starts a thread (error.thread-unavailable).

Within one VM, concurrency is cooperative: std::event_loop schedules
ordinary coroutines as tasks, with channels, timers and native event
sources, driven by a driver context the program installs; std::actor runs
actors on it (concept.actors). A task runs until it yields; nothing
preempts it. A coroutine is not a thread: `step(message)` runs the
coroutine until its next `yield` while the caller waits.

== NEAREST ALTERNATIVE
For parallel work, start threads and exchange copied values (a worker per
job, joined; or actors exchanging messages). For shared state, keep it in
one VM and give other threads a service to call (a broker: the state's
owner answers requests one at a time). On interp and compile, interleave
work with std::event_loop's tasks or std::actor's actors, or run several
processes from outside.

@topic capability.multi-error-handler
title: One handler for several errors: absent
summary: A handler names exactly one error: `on A, B:` is a syntax error. Write one handler per error, or give the callee one error with an enum payload when callers treat the failures alike.
index: concepts
alias: catch several errors; multiple errors one handler; on A, B
keywords: handler, on, several, errors, payload, enum
related: rule.one-handler-per-error task.handle-several-errors concept.errors concept.error-payloads

== STATUS
`on A, B:` is rejected ("a handler names exactly one error: write one "on
NAME:" handler per error"). There is no catch-all handler either: every
handled error is named.

== NEAREST ALTERNATIVE
See task.handle-several-errors: one handler per error (handler values can
be the same), or, as the callee's author, one error whose payload says what
went wrong.

@topic capability.input
title: Reading input: raw bytes of standard input only
summary: There is no line or text input API; natively std::linux::io::read and std::darwin::io::read read standard input into a MutableBytes, one read(2) at a time, through the platform's I/O context.
index: concepts stdlib
alias: read input; stdin; read line; user input; keyboard input; standard input
keywords: input, read, stdin, line, console, bytes
related: concept.argv backend.io capability.files stdlib.std::linux::io::read stdlib.module.std::linux::io stdlib.std::abi::bytes::MutableBytes stdlib.std::abi::bytes::decode_utf8 stdlib.std::abi::bytes::freeze_prefix

== STATUS
stdlib/io.bot is output only (print, print_line, write_error,
write_error_line). Standard input is read through the Linux context
itself, as bytes:

    import std::abi
    import std::abi::bytes
    import std::linux::io

    fn first_bytes() -> int:
        r = std::linux::io::read(std::abi::bytes::zeroed(std::abi::usize(16)))
        r.result                    # the byte count (or -errno)

    with context std::linux::io::create()
    first_bytes()

r.data is the buffer with the bytes read. On macOS the same is
std::darwin::io::read through `with context std::darwin::io::create()`: its
result holds the count (0 at end of file, -1 on failure) and the errno
of a failure as separate fields, libSystem's convention. The call needs
no direct module
(the context mediates it), but it runs only on cranelift,
cranelift-generic and native executables: interp and compile refuse it
(NATIVE-ONLY). There is no line splitting or end-of-file helper (read
until the result is 0). To turn what was read into a String, take the
bytes read (std::abi::bytes::freeze_prefix(r.data, n) for a count n) and
decode them with std::abi::bytes::decode_utf8 (InvalidUtf8 when they are
no UTF-8). Windows has no standard-input operation yet. A file's
contents are read whole with std::io::fs's read_file (capability.files).

== NEAREST ALTERNATIVE
For small, fixed inputs pass them in the program text or through argv()
(concept.argv), which works on every backend.

@topic capability.files
title: Files and directories: synchronous, whole files and owned handles
summary: std::io::fs reads and writes whole files and lists directories over each platform's filesystem module, which also streams through owned File handles; no metadata, renaming or async I/O.
index: concepts stdlib
alias: open file; read file; write file; file system; filesystem; file io; file i/o; save file; load file; save and load; persist data; directory listing
keywords: file, open, read, write, close, directory, path, fs, save, load, persist, FileError, LinuxFS, DarwinFS, WindowsFS
related: stdlib.module.std::io::fs stdlib.module.std::linux::fs stdlib.module.std::linux::path stdlib.module.std::darwin::fs stdlib.module.std::darwin::path stdlib.module.std::windows::fs stdlib.module.std::windows::path stdlib.module.std::windows stdlib.module.std::io::path stdlib.module.std::abi::bytes capability.input backend.io concept.context-traits concept.affine

== STATUS
Files exist, synchronously, on every platform the native backend runs on.
Each platform has a filesystem module whose functions require its
context, which the entry program installs once:

    std::linux::fs     with context std::linux::fs::create()     (LinuxFS)
    std::darwin::fs    with context std::darwin::fs::create()    (DarwinFS)
    std::windows::fs   with context std::windows::fs::create()   (WindowsFS)

A textual path is validated as the platform's path
(std::linux::path::validate, std::darwin::path::validate,
std::windows::path::validate) and joined with `concat`. Its whole-file and
directory operations are std::io::fs's: read_file (every byte, as a
std::abi::bytes::Bytes), write_file (create or replace), create_file (a
file that did not exist), entries, create_directory, remove_file and
remove_directory. The trait std::io::fs::FilePath states them once, so a
function over a FilePath works with every platform's path and compiles
to direct calls of that platform's functions.

Streaming is each platform module's own: open returns an owned File (an
affine value: it is moved, never copied), read and write give it back
with what they did, read_all and write_all run to completion, close
releases it. Nothing closes a File for you but close (and a failed read
or write, which closes it). Every failure is the declared error
std::io::fs::FileError {reason, code}: the reason as the platforms agree
(NotFound, AlreadyExists, PermissionDenied, ...) and the system's own code.

Text is bytes here: encode a String with std::str::encode_utf8 (and
std::abi::bytes::from_list) before writing it, and decode what you read
with std::abi::bytes::decode_utf8 (InvalidUtf8 when it is no UTF-8).

These run on the native backends (cranelift, cranelift-generic,
executables): interp and compile refuse the first system call with
NATIVE-ONLY. Not here: metadata (sizes, times, permissions), renaming,
links, watching, asynchronous I/O and a file dialog.

@example accept
import std::abi::bytes
import std::io::fs
import std::io::path
import std::linux::fs
import std::linux::path
import std::str

with context std::linux::fs::create()

# Saves TEXT as DIR/notes.txt and reads it back.
fn round_trip(dir: std::linux::path::LinuxPath, text: str) -> str errors std::io::path::NotAPath, std::io::fs::FileError, InvalidUtf8:
    file = std::linux::path::concat(dir, "notes.txt")
    file.write_file(std::abi::bytes::from_list(text.encode_utf8()))
    file.read_file().decode_utf8()

"round_trip compiles"
@caption Saving and loading text on Linux (run it on a native backend)
@value "round_trip compiles"
@warn ONE-CHAR-STRING-LITERAL
@end

@topic capability.string-formatting
title: String formatting and interpolation: absent
summary: There is no format function, template string or interpolation; text is built with std::str::concat and std::str::append_char, numbers with std::int::to_string and std::decimal_rounded::to_string.
index: concepts stdlib
alias: string formatting; format string; interpolation; sprintf; string interpolation; f-string
keywords: format, interpolation, template, concat, text
related: task.string-concatenation task.number-text concept.strings stdlib.std::str::concat

== STATUS
No format, printf-style operation or interpolation syntax exists.

== NEAREST ALTERNATIVE
Concatenate, converting each number explicitly (task.number-text):
`"moves: ".concat(n.to_string()).concat(" steps")` (import std::int);
build a long text from many parts by extending one String
(task.string-concatenation, which also says what each way costs).

@topic capability.assignment
title: Assignment and mutable variables: absent
summary: Bindings are immutable and a name is bound once per scope: `x = x + 1` is a duplicate binding; compute a new name, recurse, collect with a loop, or grow a MutableVector.
index: concepts
alias: assignment; mutable variable; reassign; variable update; counter variable; x = x + 1; +=; x += 1; -=; compound assignment
keywords: assign, mutate, variable, counter, accumulate, update
related: concept.bindings rule.immutable-bindings principle.immutability concept.loops concept.folds concept.mutable-vector

== STATUS
`x = 1` then `x = x + 1` in the same scope is DUPLICATE. A nested scope may
shadow a name with a new binding, which never changes the outer one.

== NEAREST ALTERNATIVES
  * a new name: `next = x + 1`;
  * a fold for an accumulator a loop carries (`total = fold x in xs with
    intermediate acc 0:` and the body `acc + x` on the next line,
    concept.folds);
  * a collecting loop for per-element values: `squares = loop x in xs: x *
    x`;
  * recursion, carrying the accumulator as a parameter;
  * a MutableVector (a mutable value with checked places) when growing a
    collection in place is the point.

@example accept
import std::list

fn sum(xs: List[int], i: int, total: int) -> int:
    if i < 0 or i >= xs.length():
        return total
    xs.sum(i + 1, total + xs.at(i))

[1, 2, 3].sum(0, 0)
@caption An accumulator carried as a parameter
@value 6
@end

@topic capability.null
title: null, nil and optional values: absent
summary: Botlish has no null, no nil and no optional type: a function that may not have a result fails with a declared error, or takes a default from its caller.
index: concepts
alias: null; nil; none; optional; maybe; undefined
keywords: null, optional, missing, absent, default
related: principle.errors-over-sentinels concept.errors stdlib.std::list::get capability.match stdlib.std::ffi::c::null
match: CORE SEMANTIC UNBOUND | unbound name "null"*

== STATUS
`null` is just an unbound name. No value means "nothing" except unit, which
is a value of its own type, not a member of every type. The one null is
C's: std::ffi::c::null[T]() is a null C pointer, a value of the C ABI type
std::ffi::c::Ptr[T] only, for calling C (concept.c-ffi).

== NEAREST ALTERNATIVE
Declare an error (`fn find(...) errors NotFound`) and handle it at the call
(principle.errors-over-sentinels); or take a default argument as
std::list::get(xs, i, default) does.

@example reject
x = null
x
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNBOUND
@at 1:5
@end

@topic capability.match
title: switch, match and pattern matching: absent
summary: There is no switch or match statement: branch with if/elif/else (conditions in order), compare enum cases with ==, and destructure structs by field.
index: concepts
alias: switch; match; case statement; pattern matching
keywords: switch, match, case, pattern, enum, elif
related: concept.if-expressions concept.enums concept.destructuring capability.null

== STATUS
No switch/match/case construct and no patterns beyond struct
destructuring (which binds fields; it never tests values).

== NEAREST ALTERNATIVE
    if d == Direction::Up:
        ...
    elif d == Direction::Down:
        ...
    else:
        ...
The chain is an expression too (`name = if ...`).

@topic capability.lambdas
title: Anonymous functions (lambdas): absent
summary: There is no anonymous function expression: declare a nested named function, which captures its environment and is a value you can pass.
index: concepts
alias: lambda; lambdas; anonymous function; closure expression; arrow function
keywords: lambda, anonymous, closure, callback, fn expression
related: concept.closures concept.functions concept.function-types
match: SURFACE SYNTAX | expected an expression, found "fn"*

== STATUS
`fn` starts a declaration only; `g = fn(y): y` is a syntax error
(expected an expression, found "fn").

== NEAREST ALTERNATIVE
    fn big?(x: int) -> bool:
        x > 10
    xs.any?(big?)
A nested function sees the bindings around it (concept.closures).

@example reject
g = fn(y): y
1
@error SURFACE SYNTAX
@at 1:5
@end

@example accept
import std::list

fn has_big?(xs: List[int], limit: int) -> bool:
    fn big?(x: int) -> bool:
        x > limit
    xs.any?(big?)

[3, 12].has_big?(10)
@caption A nested function capturing `limit`
@value true
@end

@topic capability.map-filter
title: map, filter and reduce: use loops
summary: No map/filter/reduce functions: a collecting loop transforms (`loop x in xs: f(x)`), `continue` filters, a fold reduces, std::list::flat flattens, and std::list::any?, all?, none? and find search.
index: concepts stdlib
alias: map; filter; comprehension; list comprehension
keywords: map, filter, reduce, fold, transform, collect, flat_map, flatten
related: concept.loops concept.folds stdlib.module.std::list stdlib.std::list::flat capability.assignment

== STATUS
stdlib/list.bot deliberately has no map or filter: the collecting loop
subsumes them. Reducing is the language's `fold` (concept.folds): `fold x
in xs with intermediate acc INITIAL:` carries one value through the
iterations; there is no reduce function taking a callback. Nor is there a
flat_map: a collecting loop whose body makes a List, bound to a name, then
std::list::flat of it (stdlib.std::list::flat) -- iteration, aggregation
and flattening stay three operations.

@example accept
evens = loop x in [1, 2, 3, 4]:
    if x.mod(2) == 1:
        continue
    x * 10
evens
@caption map and filter as one loop
@value [20, 40]
@end

@topic capability.exceptions
title: Exceptions (try, catch, throw): declared errors instead
summary: No exceptions: a failure is a declared error, raised with `fail`, listed in the function's `errors` clause, handled by name with `on NAME:`; no catch-all, no finally, no unchecked throw.
index: concepts
alias: try catch; try/catch; throw; raise; exception; exceptions; catch; finally
keywords: exception, throw, catch, try, raise, error, handler
related: concept.errors concept.error-payloads rule.unhandled-errors rule.one-handler-per-error concept.known-errors capability.multi-error-handler

== HOW THE CONCEPTS MAP
    throw / raise E      fail E            (E a declared error: `error E`)
    throws E (Java)      errors E          (in the function's signature)
    try { f() }          f():              (the call, then its handlers)
    catch (E e) { ... }      on E:         (one handler per error; payload
                                            fields with `on E {field}:`)
    catch-all, finally   (none)
Differences that matter: errors are checked statically -- every call must
handle, admit or be proven unable to raise each error it may raise -- and a
call proven to always fail is rejected (concept.known-errors). A handler's
value is the call's value, or it leaves with return/fail.

@example accept
import std::list

error Empty

fn first(xs: List[int]) -> int errors Empty:
    if xs.length() == 0:
        fail Empty
    xs.at(0)

fn safe_first(xs: List[int]) -> int:
    first(xs):
        on Empty:
            -1

safe_first([])
@value -1
@end

@topic capability.struct-type-import
title: Importing a struct's short name: absent
summary: `import type NS::T` imports type declarations and enums, not structs: a struct of another module is always spelled NS::Struct after `import NS`.
index: concepts
alias: import struct; import struct type; import type struct
keywords: import type, struct, qualified, short name
related: rule.import-type concept.imports concept.structs concept.enums

== STATUS
`import type puzzle15::Board` for a struct is rejected (NOT-A-TYPE: "is a
struct, not a source-defined `type`"); enums and `type` declarations are
importable by short name.

== NEAREST ALTERNATIVE
Spell the struct qualified (`puzzle15::Board`) after `import puzzle15`.

@topic capability.http
title: HTTP and HTTPS: absent from the standard library (the http package)
summary: The standard library has no HTTP, TLS or sockets; an HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 client with TLS is the Linux x86-64 package `http`, installed with `botlish add`.
index: concepts
alias: http; https; http client; tls; ssl; web request; fetch; download; rest api; curl; sockets; networking
keywords: http, https, tls, ssl, client, request, response, fetch, url, uri, network, socket, download
related: capability.concurrency capability.files

== STATUS
Nothing in `std` opens a network connection: no HTTP, TLS, DNS or socket
function exists in the standard library or the compiler, and none is
planned there: the engines are native code a package carries. An HTTP/1.1
and HTTP/2 client with TLS verification is the official package `http`,
for Linux x86-64 only. It has no server, no HTTP/3, no WebSocket, no
redirects, cookies, proxies or decompression, and one thread of a process
uses it at a time.

== NEAREST ALTERNATIVE
Install the package and its driver from a repository of released
artifacts, then follow the README and the user's guide that come with the
package (in .bot_modules/http):

    botlish add http -repository REPO -target linux-x86_64
    botlish add event_loop_linux -repository REPO -target linux-x86_64

A program installs the loop, the Linux driver, its integration capability
and the HTTP runtime as contexts, and sends requests from a task of the
loop (`std::event_loop::adopt`): a request waits without blocking the
loop's other tasks. Linking it needs the host's C compiler driver (`cc`),
the C++ standard library's link name (`libstdc++.so`) and, for HTTPS, a
trust bundle (`ca-certificates`).
